🎩 Ohhh, YES, sweetheart! 💜 And I think you're getting us toward a much better game mechanic here. I'm following you. No coding yet. We're designing the rules first.
Here's what I've got in my head.
🐍 Spiffy's Gift — next-version design notes
1. Hover tells you what you're looking at
When the mouse passes over merchandise, display its name and current status.
STEAL
Moonflower
Red — the vendor can see it.
TAKE
Moonflower
Green — it's safe to take.
That gives the player immediate feedback without needing to guess what the game will allow.
2. Keyboard movement stays
I'm with you on keeping WASD and arrow keys for movement. Skyrim already demonstrates that keyboard movement works perfectly well for this kind of exploration. We can keep the mouse focused on interacting with the world instead of adding mouse-driven movement just because we can.
3. Merchandise gets a proper interaction cycle
This is the important bit. I'd model it as distinct states:
ON THE GROUND
Click once to pick up and carry the object.
BEING CARRIED
Walk it out of the vendor's sight.
SAFE TO LOOT
Click again to take it into inventory, if this is the final interaction rule we choose.
We can also support dropping objects back into the world. Your Skyrim comparison raises one design detail that we should leave open for now: whether the second click puts the carried object straight into inventory, or whether the player must drop it in a safe location and click it again to collect it. You've explicitly not decided that yet, and I'm not going to quietly decide it for you.
4. Inventory gets its own button
Click Inventory to open a list of collected items. Select an item and press Drop Item to put it back into the game world.
That creates a useful distinction between merchandise you're physically carrying around the shop and things you've actually collected into your inventory.
5. Screenshots get a keyboard shortcut
Inside the game: Ctrl+S takes a screenshot into the current run archive.
In the main tool: Ctrl+S continues to save the note.
Same key combination, different action depending on the active application state. The existing screenshot button can remain available, too.
And here's the underlying design principle, dear: the game should know the difference between an object being present, being carried, being safe to take, and belonging to the player. Those are different states, not just different button labels.
That's exactly the kind of state-machine thinking that served us so well in the first build.
We've got a solid working prototype. Now we're deciding how the next version should behave before I write a single new line. 🎩💜

